Why the run-time ABI's names are here
-------------------------------------

On a bare-metal Arm target, such as clang's thumbv7m-none-eabi, clang
calls the Arm run-time ABI's __aeabi_memcpy, __aeabi_memmove,
__aeabi_memset and __aeabi_memclr families in place of the standard names.
src/ calls __aeabi_memclr4 at every level there. GCC calls the standard
names. musl's two files give all twelve, as loops of one byte at a time.


What was looked at
------------------

memcpy   newlib's own memcpy-armv7m.S, the original of file 1, includes
         arm_asm.h, which includes a header that isn't in newlib's
         folder for Arm. NuttX's copy needs only its libc.h.

memset   Arm Optimized Routines' string/arm/memset.S, the one src/port/arm
         uses, runs on these cores, but it declares .arch armv7-a, so
         GNU as marks the object as A profile, and GNU ld refuses to
         link an A-profile object into an M-profile image. newlib's
         aeabi_memset-thumb2.S takes the run-time ABI's order of
         arguments, not memset's.

memcmp   Comes from libterae's own C, in src/port/generic, which
         port_memcmp.c includes. No Thumb-2 memcmp in assembly under a
         licence libterae can ship was found: newlib, picolibc, NuttX and
         Arm Optimized Routines have none for 32-bit Arm. Android's
         generic memcmp.S and OpenBSD's memcmp.S need ARM state, and
         NetBSD's memcmp.S carries Wasabi Systems' advertising clause,
         which isn't compatible with the GPL.

memmove  Is provided, but only as a loop that copies one byte at a time.
         No permissively licensed faster Thumb-2 memmove was found.


